1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,340
 Residents in Côte d'Ineges NDG have elected a new borough mayor.

2
00:00:03,780 --> 00:00:07,520
 Stéphanie Valenzuela captured just half, 50% of the vote,

3
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:09,720
 defeating incumbent Gras-Sikosokie-Katoa.

4
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:12,420
 The new borough council is made up of three councillors

5
00:00:12,420 --> 00:00:15,140
 from Ensemble Montréal and two from Projet Montréal.

6
00:00:15,700 --> 00:00:17,340
 Stéphanie Valenzuela joins us now.

7
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:18,740
 Good morning. Congratulations.

8
00:00:19,740 --> 00:00:21,160
 Good morning. Thank you for having me.

9
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:21,900
 How are you feeling?

10
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:29,520
 To be honest, still really overwhelmed with the support

11
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:31,640
 I've been receiving in the last few days and the messages,

12
00:00:31,980 --> 00:00:34,580
 but feeling very good and very proud of the work

13
00:00:34,580 --> 00:00:36,020
 that we put in during this campaign.

14
00:00:36,620 --> 00:00:38,160
 What do you think contributed to your win?

15
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:42,840
 Really, it's the door knocking and the meeting the residents

16
00:00:42,840 --> 00:00:49,100
 and hearing everyone over the last 42 days of this campaign.

17
00:00:49,360 --> 00:00:52,800
 But even before that, when we announced our complete team in August,

18
00:00:53,680 --> 00:00:56,400
 I've been a sitting councillor for the District of Darlington

19
00:00:56,400 --> 00:00:57,640
 for the last four years.

20
00:00:58,040 --> 00:01:00,440
 And for me, in this mandate,

21
00:01:00,440 --> 00:01:04,260
 I really learned the importance of meeting with residents,

22
00:01:04,660 --> 00:01:05,400
 hearing them out.

23
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:06,340
 What are the grievances?

24
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:08,860
 What are the issues they'd like to resolve?

25
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:12,240
 And that's really what I took with me along this campaign.

26
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:15,840
 How to listen to people, how to bring solutions.

27
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:19,640
 And I think with that, the results are there.

28
00:01:19,760 --> 00:01:23,300
 It shows that that's exactly what we did in this campaign.

29
00:01:23,660 --> 00:01:25,160
 So let's talk about solutions.

30
00:01:25,420 --> 00:01:28,120
 And the next four years, what are your top priorities for the borough?

31
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:35,620
 Yeah, well, we were very clear from the very beginning when we announced our local team and our campaign.

32
00:01:35,620 --> 00:01:45,700
 We are very focused on bringing back basic services in our borough, taking care of the development of housing and the preservation of our housing stock in the borough,

33
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:48,500
 making sure that we stay affordable as much as possible.

34
00:01:48,900 --> 00:01:54,080
 And, of course, you know, security has always been top of mind in the borough of Kud and Ajandiji.

35
00:01:54,280 --> 00:01:59,380
 So these are three, you know, axes that are very clear to us that we want to make sure we prioritize.

36
00:01:59,780 --> 00:02:08,600
 But at this very moment, it's the budget of 2026 that we're already starting to work on to make sure that we have these priorities in there.

37
00:02:09,020 --> 00:02:11,560
 Let's talk about developing the housing stock.

38
00:02:11,660 --> 00:02:17,200
 One of the places where that housing stock will be developed is in the Namuribidrom site.

39
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:18,680
 Where do you think stand with that?

40
00:02:20,020 --> 00:02:31,900
 So there was two social off-market projects that were adopted in the last council with the council that we had, you know, prior to the campaign and to the election.

41
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:34,700
 So that's already something that's on the table.

42
00:02:35,220 --> 00:02:42,520
 It's clear that we need to continue discussions with all levels of government to see how the rest of the Namuribidrom site will be developed.

43
00:02:43,060 --> 00:02:46,540
 There was federal funding that came in just a few months ago.

44
00:02:46,540 --> 00:02:52,080
 So there's a whole conversation and, you know, Claude Pinard was elected in Saint-Jacques.

45
00:02:52,380 --> 00:02:58,420
 There's potentially a lot of different scenarios that are going to come in with recounting across the city.

46
00:02:58,880 --> 00:03:07,580
 But we have a plan and we have every intention of working with all the stakeholders, including the Galo and the local stakeholders that we have here in the borough.

47
00:03:07,860 --> 00:03:11,740
 What about the existing stock in Côte d'Aige and Ajandiji?

48
00:03:11,740 --> 00:03:16,700
 I know that there are some buildings, some municipal housing buildings that are not being used right now.

49
00:03:16,760 --> 00:03:18,900
 Are there any plans to do something about that?

50
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:22,060
 There are definitely plans to do something with that.

51
00:03:22,160 --> 00:03:28,700
 That was something that was also announced as one of our, you know, campaign engagements with Soraya.

52
00:03:28,980 --> 00:03:35,820
 But I'll give you a very concrete example of something that needs to advance just in terms of local housing stock.

53
00:03:35,820 --> 00:03:39,160
 There's a project called Project Chester on Walkley.

54
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:42,200
 It has been abandoned since, I believe, 2012.

55
00:03:43,580 --> 00:03:46,760
 There's potential to already start working on that project.

56
00:03:47,080 --> 00:03:49,160
 And so we need to prioritize that.

57
00:03:49,240 --> 00:03:56,820
 So that's one of the first things that I'm going to be doing coming into my seat as a borough mayor is to make sure that we can finally advance on this project.

58
00:03:57,040 --> 00:04:04,780
 If there's money that is missing, if there's, you know, funding that is needed, we need to find a way to work with our different partners to finally advance on this project.

59
00:04:05,280 --> 00:04:09,600
 Another thing that was talked about during the campaign was extending Cavendish.

60
00:04:10,420 --> 00:04:10,760
 Yes.

61
00:04:12,340 --> 00:04:15,480
 What's the plan going forward with that?

62
00:04:16,220 --> 00:04:32,900
 Well, as we had announced just a few weeks ago already towards the tail end of the campaign, our intention coming into this mandate is to make sure that we go forward with Cavendish extension by deposing our intentions through the BAP.

63
00:04:33,120 --> 00:04:34,980
 The BAP will have to analyze it.

64
00:04:35,080 --> 00:04:38,940
 That's where the last administration had stopped in terms of going forward with the project.

65
00:04:39,120 --> 00:04:41,180
 The BAP will determine the cost.

66
00:04:41,180 --> 00:04:48,280
 You know, it will determine the actual way that we can do this in a way that is not hopefully detrimental to the environment.

67
00:04:48,720 --> 00:04:54,160
 So we want to put that on the table, understand the feasibility of putting in the Cavendish extension.

68
00:04:54,540 --> 00:05:05,560
 But I think for us, our party, it's been very clear that if we're going to develop an entire site like the Numero Hippodrome site, we need to make sure that accessibility is included, security is included.

69
00:05:05,560 --> 00:05:20,800
 And with the CIM, it was very clear that you cannot develop a site with over 10,000 units and so much circulation and people going through if you don't have different access points that are safe and secure to enter the area.

70
00:05:20,980 --> 00:05:22,680
 So we're very clear on that.

71
00:05:22,880 --> 00:05:24,740
 The CIM being the Montreal Fire Department, correct?

72
00:05:25,080 --> 00:05:36,980
 And I'm curious to know, because you're talking about feasibility, if it came to that, do you see a scenario where you would ask Coat St. Luke or Town of Mount Royal to contribute?

73
00:05:36,980 --> 00:05:42,860
 Well, they've always been very close collaborators on this intention.

74
00:05:43,200 --> 00:05:49,040
 My colleague, Alan de Souza from St. Laurent, had been working with them for over, you know, decades.

75
00:05:49,340 --> 00:05:54,280
 So if it comes to a point where we have to sit down and discuss further, then for sure we will do that.

76
00:05:54,360 --> 00:05:59,560
 But for now, we have to focus on the first step, which is deposing this project at the BAP.

77
00:05:59,560 --> 00:06:08,020
 All right. Homelessness is an issue that you will have to deal with and all the elected officials at City Hall are going to have to deal with.

78
00:06:08,240 --> 00:06:10,780
 Let's talk about specifically, though, Cote Neige, NDG.

79
00:06:10,940 --> 00:06:17,040
 I know in the last few years there have been some concerns in Martin Luther King Park.

80
00:06:17,540 --> 00:06:20,840
 What's the solution that you're proposing and where do you go from there?

81
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:23,220
 Yeah, thank you for the question.

82
00:06:23,320 --> 00:06:27,020
 Actually, it's been a priority for me, even as a sitting councillor.

83
00:06:27,020 --> 00:06:31,900
 So one of the first things that we're going to do, you know, it's the month of November.

84
00:06:32,120 --> 00:06:37,660
 In a few weeks, we're going to start to feel the cold in a much different way like we do every winter.

85
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,560
 So we need to work with the stakeholders.

86
00:06:40,820 --> 00:06:52,060
 We need to find out what was already planned with this previous administration and what do we need to do to adjust to make sure that nobody is found in the park at minus 20 degrees, right?

87
00:06:52,060 --> 00:07:03,860
 So one of the things that I was able to do in my last mandate as a city councillor was depose a motion and get services put in through the partnership with a local community organization.

88
00:07:04,040 --> 00:07:05,420
 So that exists right now.

89
00:07:05,560 --> 00:07:09,520
 What we need to know is, you know, how many people have been using that site?

90
00:07:09,660 --> 00:07:16,540
 How many people are currently in the borough that need to be redirected to resources to make sure that we can identify where they all are?

91
00:07:16,820 --> 00:07:25,900
 And again, my team with Ensemble Montréal and Soraya had proposed a lot of measures for the whole city of Montreal when it comes to homelessness.

92
00:07:26,200 --> 00:07:37,620
 So there's going to be work that we need to do all together to make sure that these programs and these envelopes trickle down to all the different boroughs that are impacted in a different way with vulnerable populations.

93
00:07:37,620 --> 00:07:45,460
 One of the other big projects from the Projet Montréal era, specifically in Côte d'Inege NDG, was the Terrebonne bike path.

94
00:07:45,780 --> 00:07:47,140
 I'm curious to know what happens to that.

95
00:07:47,220 --> 00:07:48,220
 Will it stay open this winter?

96
00:07:49,620 --> 00:07:52,440
 So like we said, we're going to go into an audit.

97
00:07:52,680 --> 00:07:54,160
 So until that audit is done, the network sees...

98
00:07:54,160 --> 00:07:55,180
 That's not happening anymore, though.

99
00:07:55,400 --> 00:07:56,660
 That audit's not happening anymore.

100
00:07:57,440 --> 00:07:59,120
 It's not that it's not happening.

101
00:07:59,340 --> 00:08:03,480
 It's we have to get access to the audit that was already prepared by the administration.

102
00:08:03,480 --> 00:08:08,880
 We're going to take a look at that audit and see what was the evaluation of it.

103
00:08:09,320 --> 00:08:15,820
 And depending on the results of it and the access to it, then potentially we might not have another audit and we would use the results of that audit.

104
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:21,540
 But I think what's important is that there was already something that was done that was paid for by taxpayers.

105
00:08:22,200 --> 00:08:30,060
 And there is potentially some solutions to how we can improve and modify the current network that's there.

106
00:08:30,440 --> 00:08:35,800
 So the current position for us is that we wait for the results.

107
00:08:36,460 --> 00:08:38,420
 We keep what is there as a network.

108
00:08:38,560 --> 00:08:43,380
 So the maintenance of the Terrebonne is still very much at play here.

109
00:08:43,700 --> 00:08:51,440
 What do you say to people who were expecting that audit, who heard you during the campaign when you said that there would be an audit?

110
00:08:51,700 --> 00:08:58,380
 Even I understand that there's documents and documentation available right now and your administration is waiting to see that.

111
00:08:58,440 --> 00:09:07,860
 But what do you say to people who are waiting for you to conduct this audit and potentially looking for some solutions or some action with regards to, in your borough anyway, specifically the Taiwan bike path?

112
00:09:08,740 --> 00:09:14,940
 I would like to say that I completely understand if there is some fear that we're not going to go forward with it.

113
00:09:14,940 --> 00:09:20,840
 But I think that our responsibility is to be extremely responsible with the funds that are there.

114
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:34,280
 And if there had been already funds, which there has been spent on doing an audit, we have to do the responsible thing by taking a look at what was done, what was presented, and what had been included as solutions to this audit for the network.

115
00:09:34,440 --> 00:09:46,980
 If it isn't clear to us in the way that it was done, the audit, if it doesn't seem like it was truly done in an independent way, we have to take a look and see whether or not we have to go forward with our own audit.

116
00:09:47,300 --> 00:09:50,660
 And we have to understand that that also takes even more time, right?

117
00:09:50,760 --> 00:09:58,120
 So I think that open communication, like we've always said, and the conversation and the listening to everybody is very important.

118
00:09:58,740 --> 00:10:08,480
 And to continue giving them, you know, an état d'ilure of what's going on will hopefully reassure them that we're not just saying these things, that we're actually going to do them.

119
00:10:08,640 --> 00:10:08,740
 Right.

120
00:10:08,940 --> 00:10:18,560
 But some people do feel that, though, that you just said it, because the idea of this information being available, that was known for at least for the last little while.

121
00:10:19,380 --> 00:10:21,940
 It was known, but we didn't have access to it, right?

122
00:10:22,020 --> 00:10:23,500
 So now it's a question of having access.

123
00:10:23,640 --> 00:10:30,840
 It's a very different thing that now that we're the administration and we are able to, you know, get the studies, read the studies, do it.

124
00:10:30,920 --> 00:10:32,700
 So we're going to prioritize that.

125
00:10:32,780 --> 00:10:37,860
 And I'm sure that within our own party, a lot of the different boroughs are going to request a prioritization of it.

126
00:10:38,220 --> 00:10:43,300
 It's something that we had promised to do within the first 100 days, and we have every intention on keeping it.

127
00:10:44,040 --> 00:10:49,260
 On Borough Council in Côte d'Aneige, NDG, three districts are represented by your party, Ensemble Montréal.

128
00:10:49,460 --> 00:10:54,340
 Two other seats will be held by Projet Montréal, which is the exact opposite of the previous council.

129
00:10:54,720 --> 00:10:55,740
 How do you plan to work together?

130
00:10:57,140 --> 00:10:59,280
 I've always been a person that collaborates.

131
00:10:59,360 --> 00:11:01,440
 I did exactly that in the last four years.

132
00:11:01,560 --> 00:11:08,000
 But, you know, we were very, very close in Côte d'Aneige, so close that it went from two votes to six votes now.

133
00:11:09,220 --> 00:11:11,100
 There's potential for this to change.

134
00:11:11,240 --> 00:11:13,060
 We are going to go into a recount.

135
00:11:13,320 --> 00:11:21,540
 So regardless of what the outcome is from all of this, we will continue to work together for, you know, the interests of our residents.

136
00:11:21,540 --> 00:11:22,680
 And that's my intention.

137
00:11:22,840 --> 00:11:27,220
 It always has been regardless of what the composition of our council was going to be.

138
00:11:27,960 --> 00:11:29,500
 What do the next few days look like for you?

139
00:11:30,640 --> 00:11:40,100
 A lot of meetings, a lot of meetings to prepare for the local budget and for, you know, the swearing of the oath for us to come in into our full positions.

140
00:11:40,100 --> 00:11:54,140
 But it's just really important for me to continue meeting with the stakeholders, to continue meeting with the residents that I had the very great opportunity and privilege to meet over this campaign and to put into action everything that we said we were going to be doing.

141
00:11:54,380 --> 00:11:54,600
 All right.

142
00:11:54,640 --> 00:11:56,140
 Stephanie Valenzuela, we'll talk again soon.

143
00:11:56,220 --> 00:11:56,580
 Thank you.

144
00:11:57,300 --> 00:11:57,940
 Thank you so much.

145
00:11:58,180 --> 00:11:58,600
 Have a great day.

146
00:11:58,700 --> 00:11:58,980
 You too.

147
00:11:59,080 --> 00:12:03,160
 That is Stephanie Valenzuela, the mayor-elect of Côte d'Aneige NDG.

